\title{Mean-Force Hamiltonians from Influence Functionals}
\author{Gerard McCaul}
% \affiliation{Affiliation}
\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}
The Hamiltonian of mean force (HMF) provides the standard starting point for strong-coupling thermodynamics, yet explicit operator forms are known only in restricted settings. We present an exact influence-functional construction for a system linearly coupled to a Gaussian environment and show how a Hubbard--Stratonovich representation rewrites the reduced equilibrium state as an average over quenched imaginary-time propagators. In the commuting (pure-dephasing) sector this yields a closed operator form, $H_{\mathrm{MF}}=H_S-\lambda f^2+\mathrm{const}$, with the renormalization scale fixed by the bath kernel (equivalently the spectral density). We then validate this result against explicit finite-bath trace-out calculations and stochastic imaginary-time sampling in a five-level projector-coupled model with Ohmic bath discretization. The numerical benchmarks directly confirm the predicted $g^2$ renormalization scaling and the strong-coupling projector dominance of the reduced equilibrium state. Non-Gaussian and non-commuting extensions are deferred to follow-up work.
\end{abstract}

